The Commentary of al-Biqa'i (Nazm al-Durar) on surah 37, verse 133:

When He, glory be to Him, had completed what He intended concerning the affairs of the good-doers from the offspring of Ibrahim, peace be upon him, the messengers sent to his offspring, in consolation and in giving hope, He placed them first, because the favour upon them is a favour upon him, and a man is more delighted by his son than by his relative. They are the ones through whom Allah made manifest what was left for him among the later generations as "a tongue of truth" (lisan al-sidq). He followed them with the story of his nephew with the people of the land of Jordan (al-Urdunn), who were not of their people. So He said, emphasizing it as an alert to the victory of the believers, even if they were in few numbers and in humiliation, in a state whose recovery is not expected, and as a refutation of the Jews who denied his message or doubted it:

﴿And indeed, Lot﴾; that is, the one who stripped himself of what was familiar to him from his land and his kinfolk, by migrating with his paternal uncle Ibrahim (p-289), peace be upon them both, ﴿was surely among the messengers﴾;
